01:06:03.940 --> 01:06:07.940
And let me describe what some of these
statistics actually mean now.

01:06:07.940 --> 01:06:11.940
And I'm going to start with what we call
the measures of centrality.

01:06:11.940 --> 01:06:18.940
And all of these measures that I'm going
to talk about now basically try to
describe where the center of the data set
that we're looking at lies.

01:06:18.940 --> 01:06:22.940
So basically the values are the mean, the
mode and the median.

01:06:22.940 --> 01:06:27.940
The mean is what you guys will typically
know as the average.

01:06:27.940 --> 01:06:30.940
It's the equally weighted average of all
the data points in the series.

01:06:30.940 --> 01:06:36.940
The median is the middle number if you
were to order the data points from
smallest to largest.

01:06:36.940 --> 01:06:41.940
And the mode is the most commonly
occurring value in the data series.

01:06:41.940 --> 01:06:48.940
So as I said, all of these statistics are
calculated to give you an idea of where
the center of the data set lies.

01:06:48.940 --> 01:06:55.940
We've then got some measures of dispersion
in our values for standard deviation and
variance here.

01:06:55.940 --> 01:07:02.940
These values basically summarize the
dispersion of the data points around the
mean of the data set.

01:07:02.940 --> 01:07:08.940
And in a normally distributed set of data,
such as data on human height or weight.

01:07:08.940 --> 01:07:15.940
You typically expect to see around 68% of
the data lying within one standard
deviation from the mean.

01:07:15.940 --> 01:07:19.940
And 95% of the data within two standard
deviations from the mean.

01:07:19.940 --> 01:07:22.940
And 99% within three standard deviations.

01:07:22.940 --> 01:07:35.940
And part of what we're going to do in the
subsequent analysis is compare how our
data set here compares with those values
that we'd expect to see in a normally
distributed data set.

01:07:35.940 --> 01:07:47.940
Now, if at this stage you're not really
following what I mean about or what I mean
when I talk about normally distributed
data or you're unclear with any of the
descriptive statistics that I've been
talking about,

01:07:47.940 --> 01:07:53.940
then you'll need to have a read through of
our introduction to statistics PDF in the
download section for this video.

01:07:53.940 --> 01:07:57.940
And hopefully that gives you a better idea
of what I'm trying to say here.

01:07:57.940 --> 01:07:59.940
Let's go on to some more values now.

01:07:59.940 --> 01:08:03.940
We've got values for kurtosis and
skewness.

01:08:03.940 --> 01:08:09.940
Effectively, these values tell us how
normally the data is distributed.

01:08:09.940 --> 01:08:14.940
And Excel measures something called excess
kurtosis rather than kurtosis itself.

01:08:14.940 --> 01:08:29.940
And if the value for excess kurtosis is
higher than zero, then we know that the
distribution of our dataset is peakier
around the mean and has fatter tails than
a normally distributed dataset.

01:08:29.940 --> 01:08:41.940
Equally, if we look at our skewness value
and that's somewhere different to the
value of zero, then we know that it's not
normally distributed as well.

01:08:41.940 --> 01:08:46.940
So a skewness value of over zero is a
positive skew.

01:08:46.940 --> 01:08:53.940
And that means that a higher proportion of
the data lies in the positive tail than a
normal distribution would predict.

01:08:53.940 --> 01:09:00.940
And if it's negative, then a higher
proportion of data lies in the negative
tail than a normal distribution would
predict.

01:09:00.940 --> 01:09:08.940
And I'm going to refer back to these
statistics a little bit when we look at
the empirical distribution of our month on
month percentage change data series.

01:09:08.940 --> 01:09:12.940
So those are the key statistics that you
need to pay attention to.

01:09:12.940 --> 01:09:18.940
But there are some other useful ones here
as well to give you an idea about the sort
of range and depth of the data series.

01:09:18.940 --> 01:09:21.940
The first we've got is the count down
here.

01:09:21.940 --> 01:09:24.940
So that counts the number of data points
in the data series.

01:09:24.940 --> 01:09:28.940
We've also got values for the minimum, the
maximum and the range.

01:09:28.940 --> 01:09:31.940
Okay, they should be fairly self
explanatory.

01:09:31.940 --> 01:09:35.940
The minimum is the lowest value in the
data series.

01:09:35.940 --> 01:09:37.940
The maximum is the highest value.

01:09:37.940 --> 01:09:40.940
And the range is the difference between
those two values.

01:09:40.940 --> 01:09:46.940
Okay, so that's our descriptive statistics
